.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/uri-js Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "URI\&.js"
.PP
URI\&.js is an \fRRFC 3986\fP compliant, scheme extendable URI parsing/validating/resolving library for all JavaScript environments (browsers, Node\&.js, etc)\&. It is also compliant with the IRI (\fRRFC 3987\fP), IDNA (\fRRFC 5890\fP), IPv6 Address (\fRRFC 5952\fP), IPv6 Zone Identifier (\fRRFC 6874\fP) specifications\&.

.PP
URI\&.js has an extensive test suite, and works in all (Node\&.js, web) environments\&. It weighs in at 6\&.4kb (gzipped, 17kb deflated)\&.
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "Parsing"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.parse("uri://user:pass@example\&.com:123/one/two\&.three?q1=a1&q2=a2#body");
//returns:
//{
//  scheme : "uri",
//  userinfo : "user:pass",
//  host : "example\&.com",
//  port : 123,
//  path : "/one/two\&.three",
//  query : "q1=a1&q2=a2",
//  fragment : "body"
//}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Serializing"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.serialize({scheme : "http", host : "example\&.com", fragment : "footer"}) === "http://example\&.com/#footer"
.fi
.PP
.SS "Resolving"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.resolve("uri://a/b/c/d?q", "\&.\&./\&.\&./g") === "uri://a/g"
.fi
.PP
.SS "Normalizing"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.normalize("HTTP://ABC\&.com:80/%7Esmith/home\&.html") === "http://abc\&.com/~smith/home\&.html"
.fi
.PP
.SS "Comparison"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.equal("example://a/b/c/%7Bfoo%7D", "eXAMPLE://a/\&./b/\&.\&./b/%63/%7bfoo%7d") === true
.fi
.PP
.SS "IP Support"
.PP
.nf
//IPv4 normalization
URI\&.normalize("//192\&.068\&.001\&.000") === "//192\&.68\&.1\&.0"

//IPv6 normalization
URI\&.normalize("//[2001:0:0DB8::0:0001]") === "//[2001:0:db8::1]"

//IPv6 zone identifier support
URI\&.parse("//[2001:db8::7%25en1]");
//returns:
//{
//  host : "2001:db8::7%en1"
//}
.fi
.PP
.SS "IRI Support"
.PP
.nf
//convert IRI to URI
URI\&.serialize(URI\&.parse("http://examplé\&.org/rosé")) === "http://xn--exampl-gva\&.org/ros%C3%A9"
//convert URI to IRI
URI\&.serialize(URI\&.parse("http://xn--exampl-gva\&.org/ros%C3%A9"), {iri:true}) === "http://examplé\&.org/rosé"
.fi
.PP
.SS "Options"
All of the above functions can accept an additional options argument that is an object that can contain one or more of the following properties:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRscheme\fP (string)

.PP
Indicates the scheme that the URI should be treated as, overriding the URI's normal scheme parsing behavior\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreference\fP (string)

.PP
If set to \fR"suffix"\fP, it indicates that the URI is in the suffix format, and the validator will use the option's \fRscheme\fP property to determine the URI's scheme\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtolerant\fP (boolean, false)

.PP
If set to \fRtrue\fP, the parser will relax URI resolving rules\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRabsolutePath\fP (boolean, false)

.PP
If set to \fRtrue\fP, the serializer will not resolve a relative \fRpath\fP component\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRiri\fP (boolean, false)

.PP
If set to \fRtrue\fP, the serializer will unescape non-ASCII characters as per \fRRFC 3987\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRunicodeSupport\fP (boolean, false)

.PP
If set to \fRtrue\fP, the parser will unescape non-ASCII characters in the parsed output as per \fRRFC 3987\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdomainHost\fP (boolean, false)

.PP
If set to \fRtrue\fP, the library will treat the \fRhost\fP component as a domain name, and convert IDNs (International Domain Names) as per \fRRFC 5891\fP\&.
.PP
.SH "Scheme Extendable"
.PP
URI\&.js supports inserting custom \fRscheme\fP dependent processing rules\&. Currently, URI\&.js has built in support for the following schemes:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
http [\fRRFC 2616\fP]
.IP "\(bu" 2
https [\fRRFC 2818\fP]
.IP "\(bu" 2
ws [\fRRFC 6455\fP]
.IP "\(bu" 2
wss [\fRRFC 6455\fP]
.IP "\(bu" 2
mailto [\fRRFC 6068\fP]
.IP "\(bu" 2
urn [\fRRFC 2141\fP]
.IP "\(bu" 2
urn:uuid [\fRRFC 4122\fP]
.PP
.SS "HTTP/HTTPS Support"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.equal("HTTP://ABC\&.COM:80", "http://abc\&.com/") === true
URI\&.equal("https://abc\&.com", "HTTPS://ABC\&.COM:443/") === true
.fi
.PP
.SS "WS/WSS Support"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.parse("wss://example\&.com/foo?bar=baz");
//returns:
//{
//  scheme : "wss",
//  host: "example\&.com",
//  resourceName: "/foo?bar=baz",
//  secure: true,
//}

URI\&.equal("WS://ABC\&.COM:80/chat#one", "ws://abc\&.com/chat") === true
.fi
.PP
.SS "Mailto Support"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.parse("mailto:alpha@example\&.com,bravo@example\&.com?subject=SUBSCRIBE&body=Sign%20me%20up!");
//returns:
//{
//  scheme : "mailto",
//  to : ["alpha@example\&.com", "bravo@example\&.com"],
//  subject : "SUBSCRIBE",
//  body : "Sign me up!"
//}

URI\&.serialize({
    scheme : "mailto",
    to : ["alpha@example\&.com"],
    subject : "REMOVE",
    body : "Please remove me",
    headers : {
        cc : "charlie@example\&.com"
    }
}) === "mailto:alpha@example\&.com?cc=charlie@example\&.com&subject=REMOVE&body=Please%20remove%20me"
.fi
.PP
.SS "URN Support"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.parse("urn:example:foo");
//returns:
//{
//  scheme : "urn",
//  nid : "example",
//  nss : "foo",
//}
.fi
.PP
.SS "URN UUID Support"
.PP
.nf
URI\&.parse("urn:uuid:f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6");
//returns:
//{
//  scheme : "urn",
//  nid : "uuid",
//  uuid : "f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6",
//}
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
To load in a browser, use the following tag: 
.PP
.nf
<script type="text/javascript" src="uri-js/dist/es5/uri\&.all\&.min\&.js"></script>

.fi
.PP

.PP
To load in a CommonJS/Module environment, first install with npm/yarn by running on the command line: 
.PP
.nf
npm install uri-js
# OR
yarn add uri-js

.fi
.PP

.PP
Then, in your code, load it using: 
.PP
.nf
const URI = require("uri-js");

.fi
.PP

.PP
If you are writing your code in ES6+ (ESNEXT) or TypeScript, you would load it using: 
.PP
.nf
import * as URI from "uri-js";

.fi
.PP

.PP
Or you can load just what you need using named exports: 
.PP
.nf
import { parse, serialize, resolve, resolveComponents, normalize, equal, removeDotSegments, pctEncChar, pctDecChars, escapeComponent, unescapeComponent } from "uri-js";

.fi
.PP
.SH "Breaking changes"
.PP
.SS "Breaking changes from 3\&.x"
URN parsing has been completely changed to better align with the specification\&. Scheme is now always \fRurn\fP, but has two new properties: \fRnid\fP which contains the Namspace Identifier, and \fRnss\fP which contains the Namespace Specific String\&. The \fRnss\fP property will be removed by higher order scheme handlers, such as the UUID URN scheme handler\&.

.PP
The UUID of a URN can now be found in the \fRuuid\fP property\&.
.SS "Breaking changes from 2\&.x"
URI validation has been removed as it was slow, exposed a vulnerabilty, and was generally not useful\&.
.SS "Breaking changes from 1\&.x"
The \fRerrors\fP array on parsed components is now an \fRerror\fP string\&. 
